              '03.2': { type: 'exact',
                points: ["De Morgan's (Law)", "De Morgan's", 'De Morgan'],
                guidance: "1 mark for De Morgan's Law." },
              '03.3': { type: 'points', points: ['Final answer: A·B̄ + C (1 mark)', 'Working (max 3, one mark per application): a successful application of De Morgan\'s Law (and any associated cancellation of NOTs) that produces a simpler expression', 'Applying an identity other than cancelling NOTs that produces a simpler expression (e.g. X + 1 = 1, X·1 = X, X + X̄ = 1)', 'Successfully putting terms into brackets', 'Successfully expanding brackets', 'Successfully using the distributive law'], guidance: '4 marks (AO2). 1 mark for final answer A·B̄ + C. Max 3 for working: award one mark per application of the listed techniques. The two sub-expressions ¬(Ā + B·C + B·C̄) and C·(A + Ā·(B + 1)) are marked independently: stop awarding marks for a sub-expression as soon as a mistake is made in it, but continue to award marks for simplifying the other. Award marks for working until an incorrect step has been made; ignore missing steps as long as the jumps between steps are logically correct; if different parts are simplified simultaneously in one step, award all relevant marks for that step but no further marks for parts simplified incorrectly. A simpler expression is one that is logically equivalent to the original but uses fewer logical operators. Max 3 overall if any working is incorrect. Example solution: C·(A + Ā·(B+1)) → C·(A + Ā·1) [X+1=1] → C·(A + Ā) [X·1=X] → C·1 [X+X̄=1] → C [X·1=X]; ¬(Ā + B·C + B·C̄) → ¬(Ā + B·(C + C̄)) [put into brackets] → ¬(Ā + B·1) [X+X̄=1] → ¬(Ā + B) [X·1=X] → A·B̄ [De Morgan]; answer A·B̄ + C.' },
