               {
                "code": "1(a)",
                "preamble": "Source 1(a) (summary): Adapted from an Institute for Government comment piece on Labour’s plans for House of Lords reform; it explores the role of the unelected House of Lords in UK democracy and sets out two opposing views. View 1 – the Lords plays a key role in the constitution: it is an essential revising chamber, giving detailed scrutiny to legislation and government activity, and has increasingly become a key check on government by challenging legislation that passed easily through the Commons. It can do this because it is unelected: an appointed House brings greater independence, no government majority, expertise, and more time for members to spend on scrutiny. Making the Lords elected could upset the constitutional balance, first because an elected Lords might share the Commons’ weaknesses and be less able to check government, and second because a clash could develop over whether the Commons or an elected Lords is more legitimate. View 2 – the unelected Lords is indefensible: it lacks the democratic mandate to do its job effectively. It is not full of specialists but packed with party donors, lobbyists and wealthy, well-connected people; appointments have become more numerous and inappropriate, and many new peers rarely contribute. Hereditary peers and religious representatives are unsuitable in a modern democracy, and attempts to limit the size and cost of the 800-member chamber have had little effect. The Lords needs electoral legitimacy, should be much smaller and should better represent the UK’s regions and nations while keeping its existing powers; it could then check government effectively without challenging the authority of the Commons. (This is a summary. The full source is in the official question paper.)",
                "prompt": "Using the source, evaluate the view that there should be an elected House of Lords.\n\nIn your response you must:\n• compare and contrast different opinions in the source\n• examine and debate these views in a balanced way\n• analyse and evaluate only the information presented in the source.",
                "marks": 30
               },
               {
                "code": "1(b)",
                "preamble": "Source 1(b) (summary): A passage assessing how effective constitutional reform has been since 1997, setting successes against criticisms in four areas. Devolution: introduced by Tony Blair’s Labour government and extended by later governments, it can be seen as a success in decentralising power and increasing democracy; the Scottish Parliament, Welsh Senedd and Northern Ireland Assembly keep majority support, and although some places do not want city mayors, Metro Mayors look set to stay. But it has failed in its main aim of rebuilding enthusiasm for the UK and limiting calls for independence; since Brexit it seems even more unsettled, and the process is unequal and unfair, giving England no clear representation or voice. Elections: different electoral systems for the devolved bodies have increased their legitimacy, though turnout has been a problem. The Elections Act 2022 introduced voter ID to tackle fraud and build trust, but others see it as tackling a non-existent problem that may reduce turnout. Human Rights Act 1998: it has raised awareness and protection of rights and been an effective check on over-powerful government, but it remains controversial; some politicians and parts of the media argue it protects terrorists and dangerous criminals and want reform to balance individual rights, national security and effective government. Constitutional Reform Act 2005: it created a separation of powers and more effective checks and balances, but the Supreme Court has been criticised for overstepping its role and becoming too political, and some see judicial review, though needed, as overused. (This is a summary. The full source is in the official question paper.)",
                "prompt": "Using the source, evaluate the view that constitutional reform since 1997 has been a success.\n\nIn your response you must:\n• compare and contrast different opinions in the source\n• examine and debate these views in a balanced way\n• analyse and evaluate only the information presented in the source.",
                "marks": 30
               }
