            questions: [
             {
              "number": 1,
              "scenario": "SECTION A: Short questions. Answer Questions 01, 02 and 03.",
              "parts": [
               {
                "code": "01",
                "prompt": "Explain and analyse three ways in which judicial independence is upheld in the UK.",
                "marks": 9
               },
               {
                "code": "02",
                "prompt": "Explain and analyse three influences upon individual MPs when voting in the House of Commons.",
                "marks": 9
               },
               {
                "code": "03",
                "prompt": "Explain and analyse three circumstances under which referendums have been held in the UK.",
                "marks": 9
               }
              ]
             },
             {
              "number": 2,
              "scenario": "SECTION B: Extract question. How democratic is interest group activity in the UK? Read the extract, then answer Question 04.\n\nExtract 1 (summary): adapted from a report in the 2018 Audit of UK democracy by Democratic Audit, an independent research unit in the Government Department of the London School of Economics. It sets out the pluralist view that many groups can compete on equal terms for power and influence. Elections tell policy makers little about public opinion, whereas joining a pressure group shows commitment to a cause and can lend a group legitimacy with politicians; some groups have millions of members, and striking or demonstrating shows even greater commitment. Interest groups let citizens influence decision makers between elections and supply them with useful, fairly reliable information, and parliamentary procedures give groups openings to take part. To keep their influence, however, groups need to act responsibly, lawfully and without confrontation. The extract then turns to inequality of influence: governments have often ignored some groups while others enjoy easy access. Elite groups can effectively buy political power; ITV political editor Robert Peston is cited as saying politicians regularly listen to the super-rich. Some groups can hire professional lobbyists, and the American academic Charles Lindblom warned of the privileged position of business. MPs and peers may take outside jobs and money from well-funded interests, which has produced a series of scandals and some limits on lobbying.\n\n(This is a summary. The full extract is in the official question paper.)",
              "parts": [
               {
                "code": "04",
                "prompt": "Analyse, evaluate and compare the arguments in the above extract regarding the advantages and disadvantages of the roles played by interest groups within pluralist democracy in the UK.",
                "marks": 25,
                "extended": true
               }
              ]
             },
             {
              "number": 3,
              "scenario": "SECTION C: Essay question. Answer EITHER Question 05 OR Question 06. In your answer you should draw on material from across the whole range of your course of study in Politics.",
              "parts": [
               {
                "code": "05",
                "prompt": "‘The United Kingdom now has a multi-party system.’ Analyse and evaluate this statement.",
                "marks": 25,
                "extended": true
               },
               {
                "code": "06",
                "prompt": "‘Party leaders are of decisive importance in the winning or losing of general elections.’ Analyse and evaluate this statement.",
                "marks": 25,
                "extended": true
               }
              ]
             }
            ],
